%The Colombian Central Collection of potatoes (CCC) represents one of the most diverse germplasm collections in the country and serves as the primary source of genetic variability for potato breeding in Colombia.
 This study uses historical color trait data to dissect the genetic architecture of coloration across different organs of the potato plant in landraces of the underexplored Andigenum group,
 employing Genome-Wide Association Studies (GWAS) and genomic prediction.
 GWAS analysis identified 21 significant SNPs associated with five traits:
 ten related to the secondary color of the tuber flesh,
 four to the primary and secondary colors of the flower,
 three to the Primary Color of the Flower,
 and two to the Secondary Color of the sprout.
 Moreover,
 21 SNP markers were annotated with 21 genes,
 represented by a total of 33 transcripts.
 Half of the SNPs had support from previous color or nutritional related studies.
 Overall,
 color trait heritability reached its highest values for tuber flesh secondary color (0.98) and flower primary color (0.84).
 Genomic prediction yielded predictive abilities of up to 0.84 for flower primary color and 0.71 for stem color.
 We concluded that color in the Andigenum potato group shows strong heritability,
 useful for future breeding studies and the promotion of some local materials for increasing food security in Colombia.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

