Color traits were documented for stems (seven categories),
 berries (seven categories),
 primary and secondary flower colors (eight and nine categories,
 respectively),
 tuber skin (nine and ten categories,
 respectively),
 tuber flesh (eight and nine categories,
 respectively),
 and sprouts (five and six categories,
 respectively),
 following the descriptors proposed by 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "gomez2000guia"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 The 599 accessions were evaluated over 3 years (2015–2017) under similar environmental conditions because of the large size of the 
\shape italic
Solanum tuberosum Andigenum
\shape default
 collection and the limited personnel.
 Each trait was fully evaluated within a given year,
 although different traits were assessed in different years.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From 2017 to 2019,
 color characterization was further refined using the fifth edition of the Royal Horticultural Society (RHS) color chart 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{voss2002royal}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 under comparable environmental conditions.
 Each trait was evaluated within one year.
 This update expanded the number of codes from a maximum of 10 to 884,
 enabling a more detailed characterization of the same nine traits:
 stem color (StemC),
 berry color (BerryC),
 primary flower color (PCFlower),
 primary tuber skin color (PCTuberskin),
 secondary tuber skin color (SCTuberskin),
 primary tuber flesh color (PCTuberflesh),
 secondary tuber flesh color (SCTuberflesh),
 primary sprout color (PCSprout),
 and secondary sprout color (SCSprout).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Moreover,
 color characterization using the RHS color chart was converted to the Hue,
 Chroma,
 and Lightness (HCL or LCH) color space,
 a model closely aligned with human color perception and previously applied in potato color studies 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{caraza2020image}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The values of these LCH traits were derived from the transformation of the values of the potato color traits,
 measured using the Royal Horticultural Society (RHS) fifth edition color chart (http://rhscf.orgfree.com/) 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{voss2002royal}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 into the three components:
 L,
 C,
 and H,
 of the Commission Internationale de l'Éclairage (CIE) LCH or CIE*L*C*h color space.
 Consequently,
 three components were obtained for each potato color trait,
 which are referred to as LCH traits.
 Thus,
 the nine potato color traits were transformed into 27 LCH traits that were used in the genomic analyses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\color blue
Outlier assessment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color blue
Prior to downstream analyses,
 outliers were evaluated independently for each LCH color trait component.
 Outlier detection was performed using Tukey’s interquartile range (IQR) criterion,
 as implemented in R,
 where values below Q1 − 1.5×IQR or above Q3 + 1.5×IQR are classified as outliers.
 This assessment was applied to all traits and components.
 Although a small number of extreme values were identified by this criterion,
 these observations corresponded to biologically plausible color phenotypes and were therefore retained for all subsequent analyses.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Correlation between two color descriptors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The color characteristics of CCC were assessed in different years using two descriptors that contained different numbers of codes.
 These evaluations were based on field collections conducted under comparable environmental conditions within the same municipality at an elevation of 2,950 m above sea level.
 For each of the nine color traits,
 the relationship between the two descriptor evaluations was determined using Cramér’s V-statistic 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{Lyman1986}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Cramér’s V measures the strength of the association between two qualitative variables,
 with values ranging from 0 to 1.
 Values below 0.6 indicate a weak association,
 whereas values of 0.6 or higher indicate a medium to strong association.
 To study the genetics underlying color,
 we selected traits with a medium-to-strong association,
 indicating a genetic influence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Linking Color to Nutritional and Nutraceutical Variables
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This study also examined the association between color traits and nutritional and nutraceutical values using data from a previous study,
 in which specific methodologies for quantifying these traits have been described 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{berdugo2023phenotypic}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The nutritional traits of the potatoes were represented by the Ascorbic Acid Content (AAC),
 whereas the nutraceutical traits were the Total Phenol Content (TPC) and Antioxidant Activity (measured by 2.2-Diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl Radical Scavenging activity (DPPH) and Ferric Reducing Antioxidant Power (FRAP) assays.
 Because the nutritional and nutraceutical variables were continuous and the color traits were categorical,
 the coefficient of determination was used as a measure of association 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{nagelkerke1991note}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 These analyses were conducted on 282 
\shape italic
Andigenum
\shape default
 accessions from the CCC,
 which contained data for both trait categories.
\end_layout

