\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Candidate Marker Identification 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "score"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To integrate the results from multiple traits and the two GWAS tools implemented in MultiGWAS,
 we developed a proprietary scoring function called 
\begin_inset Formula $GSCORE$
\end_inset

 to rank the markers.
 For each tool,
 the top 50 markers with the lowest p-values were selected,
 yielding 100 candidate markers (two tools × fifty markers).
 
\begin_inset Formula $GSCORE$
\end_inset

 is defined as the weighted sum of three components:
 the inflation factor (
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

,
 70%),
 replicability (
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 10%),
 and significance (
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

,
 20%),
 as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Formula $GSCORE(M)=0.7*I+0.1*R+0.2*S$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here,
 
\begin_inset Formula $I=1-|1-\lambda(M)|$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(M)$
\end_inset

 is the inflation factor for marker 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 with values closest to one yielding the highest score.
 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 represents the number of SNPs replicated across the GWAS tools and 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is a binary value (1 if the SNP is significant,
 and 0 otherwise).
 Markers achieve high 
\begin_inset Formula $GSCORE$
\end_inset

 values when they exhibit appropriate inflation,
 reproducibility across tools,
 and statistical significance.
 This scoring system prioritizes robust,
 consistent,
 and biologically meaningful markers.
\end_layout

