\begin_layout Subsection
Heritability Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Narrow-sense heritability (
\begin_inset Formula $h^{2}$
\end_inset

),
 defined as the proportion of phenotypic variance explained by additive genetic effects 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{Campos2015}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 was estimated for each of the 21 LCH components derived from color traits.
 We used Bayesian Ridge Regression (BRR),
 implemented in the 
\family typewriter
BGLR
\family default
 R package v1.1.4 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{Campos2014}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 because the color-related traits analyzed in this study are expected to follow a polygenic architecture,
 with many loci of small effects contributing to the phenotype.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
BRR assumes normally distributed genetic effects across markers and estimates heritability by repeatedly sampling additive genetic variance (
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

) and residual variance (
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{e}^{2}$
\end_inset

) through a Bayesian process,
 as follows:
 The model was run for 15,000 iterations,
 with a burn-in of 5,000 iterations and thinning every five steps.
 Heritability was computed at each iteration as:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h^{2}=\dfrac{\sigma_{a}^{2}}{\sigma_{a}^{2}+\sigma_{e}^{2}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This procedure yields a posterior distribution of the heritability values for each trait,
 rather than a single-point estimate.
 From this distribution,
 we report the posterior mean and 95% credible interval.
 Unlike confidence intervals from frequentist methods,
 a Bayesian 95% credible interval indicates the range within which the true 
\begin_inset Formula $h{^{2}}$
\end_inset

 value lies with 95% probability,
 given the data and model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Genomic Prediction (GP)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Genomic Prediction (GP) was applied to estimate the genetic potential of individuals for each of the 21 LCH color trait components.
 Seven statistical models were used,
 including parametric,
 semiparametric,
 and Bayesian approaches.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Parametric
\series default
:
 Genomic Best Linear Unbiased Prediction (GBLUP) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Semiparametric
\series default
:
 Reproducing Kernel Hilbert Spaces (RKHS) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Bayesian
\series default
:
 Bayesian Ridge Regression (BRR),
 Bayesian LASSO (BL),
 Bayes A,
 Bayes B,
 and Bayes C 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All models were implemented using the 
\family typewriter
BWGS
\family default
 R package v0.2.1 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{Charmet2020}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 The predictive performance was assessed via 5-fold cross-validation,
 repeated five times for each trait.
 The predictive ability was measured as the Pearson correlation between the observed phenotypes and genomic estimated breeding values (GEBVs) across the validation folds.
 All model parameters were set to the default values provided by the BWGS library.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
GP using marker subsets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To determine the optimal number of markers for the genomic prediction (GP) of each LCH color trait component,
 GP was performed using subsets containing between 1% and 100% of the markers.
 Markers were selected based on the GWAS results and prioritized using our custom 
\begin_inset Formula $GSCORE$
\end_inset

