\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "score"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

) to assess whether the predictive ability could be improved with a reduced marker set size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All traits displayed distinct distribution patterns across the three components,
 with varying amounts of available data (Supplementary Figure S2).
 Component H showed the widest distribution for eight of the nine traits,
 whereas Components L and C exhibited similar ranges for seven of the nine traits and displayed bimodal distributions for five of them,
 including both tuber skin color traits.
 Berry color showed the narrowest distribution (0–120) across all three components,
 whereas the remaining eight traits spanned a broader range (0–350).
 As no outliers were detected,
 all available data were retained for analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Data availability analysis revealed that seven of the nine traits had complete records for > 90% of the accessions.
 The most limited coverage was observed for secondary color traits,
 particularly secondary tuber flesh color (n = 94),
 followed by secondary tuber skin color (n = 473).
 This reflects the natural absence of secondary pigmentation in many accessions,
 which requires full characterization.
 Because field evaluations were conducted 2,950 m above sea level,
 it is possible that higher-altitude trials might reveal additional cases of secondary coloration,
 as elevated altitudes are known to enhance anthocyanin and flavonoid production 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{qi2023anthocyanin,flavonoids}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

