\backslash
end{landscape}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notably,
 chromosome 2 accounted for 28.5% of the significant SNPs,
 followed by chromosomes 1 and 11,
 contributing 19% and 14.29%,
 respectively,
 respectively.
 Two chromosomes with the highest number of significant SNPs have previously been reported to harbor loci involved in potato pigmentation,
 with chromosomes 2 and 11 linked to the synthesis of red and blue anthocyanins 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{van1993identification,riveros2022uncovering}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

%
\backslash
subsubsection{Previous color and nutritional studies}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
One associated SNP was located within the dihydroflavonol reductase gene DFR (DM.02G024900) at the R locus,
 which controls the production of red anthocyanins 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{de2003allele,riveros2022uncovering}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:GWAS-Top-Markers-GSCORE"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

B).
 In addition,
 a gene on chromosome 2,
 located near both DFR and Flavanone-3-hydroxylase (F3H;
 DM.02G024500),
 was associated with the secondary color of the tuber flesh and may form part of a co-regulatory region.
 A locus on chromosome 2 near 45 Mb has also been previously associated with tuber skin brightness 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{sharma2024genotyping}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 secondary tuber flesh color 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{Berdugo2017}
\end_layout

\end_inset

,
 and Total Phenol content 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{berdugo2023phenotypic}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Chromosome 2 harbors multiple loci linked to color traits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On chromosome 1,
 we detected a locus associated with the secondary color of the tuber flesh,
 previously reported to be involved in anthocyanin biosynthesis and pigmentation regulation via a WD40-like regulatory protein (DM.01G036130) 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{liu2020wd40,
 sajeevan2023comprehensive}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 Furthermore,
 an SNP on chromosome 3 at 
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

55 Mb corresponds to a major QTL reported for purple skin and tuber flesh color 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{pandey2021genetic,
 sharma2024genotyping}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Additional loci have been found in proximity to QTLs for other tuber-related traits,
 such as eye depth,
 average tuber depth,
 tuber yield,
 and skin brightness 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{sharma2024genotyping,pandey2022genomic}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 These results confirm that the genes involved in coloration in the 
\shape italic
Andigenum
\shape default
 group are consistent with those of previous potato studies,
 including loci linked to nutraceutical traits 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
citep{berdugo2023phenotypic,riveros2022uncovering,parra2019chromosomes,parra2021pathway}
\end_layout

\end_inset

.
 However,
 42.8% of the SNPs identified were not reported in earlier studies,
 highlighting the need to further explore the 
\shape italic
Andigenum
\shape default
 group.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Moreover,
 GWASpoly and GAPIT identified 14 and 12 significant SNPs,
 respectively,
 with six SNPs jointly detected by both tools,
 representing 28.57% of the total.
 Although no SNPs were shared across different traits,
 four SNPs were associated with distinct LCH components within the same trait.
 A Circos plot (Figure S3) combined the data for the five traits and presented the density of SNP markers for chromosomes,
 allowing the visualization of SNP co-occurrence across different traits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Heritability of Color Traits
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The posterior distributions of narrow-sense heritability (
\begin_inset Formula $h^{2}$
\end_inset

) for 21 LCH components across seven color traits in the 
\shape italic
Andigenum
\shape default
 group of 
\shape italic
Solanum tuberosum
\shape default
 are shown as boxplots,
 which display the median,
 interquartile range,
 and 95% credible interval of 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{2}$
\end_inset

 values estimated using Bayesian Ridge Regression (BRR) (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Heritabilities_and_Abilities"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 A).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Most primary color traits,
 particularly 
\family typewriter
PCFlower.H
\family default
,
 
\family typewriter
StemC.L
\family default
,
 and 
\family typewriter
PCSprout.C
\family default
,
 exhibited moderate to high heritability,
 with means exceeding 0.55.
 The highest heritability was observed for the hue component of the secondary tuber flesh color (
\family typewriter
SCTuberflesh.H
\family default
),
 with a mean of approximately 0.98,
 indicating strong genetic control.
 In contrast,
 several secondary color traits,
 such as 
\family typewriter
SCSprout.C
\family default
,
 
\family typewriter
SCSprout.H/L
\family default
,
 and 
\family typewriter
SCTuberskin.C
\family default
,
 displayed lower heritability values (posterior means between 0.20 and 0.35),
 suggesting a greater influence of environmental or nonadditive factors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stem,
 primary flower,
 and primary sprout color traits showed consistent moderate to high heritability values across the HCL components,
 supporting their utility in genomic selection and marker-assisted breeding strategies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Genomic Prediction Results
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The highest predictive abilities (mean correlations 
\begin_inset Formula $>$
\end_inset

 0.70) were obtained for 
\family typewriter
PCFlower.H
\family default
,
 
\family typewriter
StemC.L
\family default
,
 and 
\family typewriter
PCSprout.C
\family default
,
 whereas other primary color traits,
 such as 
\family typewriter
PCTuberskin.C
\family default
 and 
\family typewriter
PCSprout.L
\family default
,
 showed moderate performance (Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Heritabilities_and_Abilities"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

 B).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast,
 the lowest predictive abilities were observed for secondary color traits,
 particularly 
\family typewriter
SCSprout.H
\family default
,
 
\family typewriter
SCTuberskin.C
\family default
,
 and 
\family typewriter
SCTuberflesh.L
\family default
,
 all with mean correlations below 0.40.
 These results are consistent with the heritability patterns shown in Figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Heritabilities_and_Abilities"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"

\end_inset

A,
 where traits with higher heritability generally achieved greater predictive accuracies.
 A notable exception was the secondary tuber flesh color,
 which performed poorly because of the limited dataset (94 accessions) and the cross-validation scheme used,
 resulting in predictive values below 0.35 across all three color components.
 The performance also varied across models,
 with BRR,
 GBLUP,
 and RKHS outperforming each other,
 depending on the trait category (Figure 
