\begin_layout Subsubsection
GP using marker subsets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Genomic prediction was performed using marker subsets representing 1–100% of the 4,641 SNPs.
 The predictive ability,
 assessed by the Pearson correlation between observed phenotypes and genomic estimated breeding values (GEBVs),
 varied with traits and marker densities (Figure S4).
 In general,
 the predictive ability increased rapidly with marker density up to 40%-50% of the total set,
 after which the gains plateaued,
 indicating diminishing returns.
 This pattern was observed across most color traits and LCH components (Lightness,
 Chroma,
 Hue),
 suggesting that a moderate number of well-distributed,
 informative SNPs can achieve a predictive performance comparable to that of the full marker set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stem color and primary flower color consistently showed higher predictive abilities,
 with correlations approaching 0.75 at the full marker density.
 In contrast,
 the secondary traits with lower data availability (Fig.
 S2),
 such as secondary tuber flesh (n=94),
 secondary tuber skin color (n=473),
 and secondary sprout color (n=542),
 exhibited lower predictive values.
 Secondary color results may still be influenced by the cross-validation strategy and small sample sizes,
 even when larger marker sets are used.
 This variability highlights differences in genetic architecture,
 with some traits likely governed by major loci and others shaped by more polygenic and complex effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These results demonstrate the feasibility of cost-effective marker-assisted breeding for several color traits in tetraploid Andigenum potatoes using an optimized SNP subset.
 The top 50% of the markers selected with the 
\begin_inset Formula $GSCORE$
\end_inset

 metric (Section 2.3) effectively captured the relevant genetic variation,
 allowing accurate predictions while reducing computational requirements.
\end_layout

