\begin_layout Subsection
Limitations and Future Directions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color blue
Our study was conducted at a single altitude with fixed environmental conditions for each trait evaluation,
 which limits our ability to fully disentangle genotype-by-environment (GxE) interactions.
 However,
 we employed multiple strategies to assess the relative contributions of genetic versus environmental factors.
 We selected traits showing high correlation (
\begin_inset Formula $>$
\end_inset

0.6) between independent evaluations conducted in different years,
 reasoning that weak correlations would indicate predominant environmental effects.
 Broad-sense heritability estimates revealed substantial variation across traits,
 ranging from 
\begin_inset Formula $>$
\end_inset

0.6 (indicating strong genetic control) to 
\begin_inset Formula $<$
\end_inset

0.2 (suggesting minimal genetic influence),
 with intermediate values (0.3-0.6) indicating both genetic and environmental contributions.
 While our findings identify genomic regions associated with color traits under these specific conditions,
 the genetic architecture of these traits may differ under alternative environmental conditions.
 Evaluations across altitude gradients would be valuable for identifying additional loci and quantifying GxE effects,
 potentially revealing environmentally-dependent genetic mechanisms not captured in the current study.
 Such multi-environment trials would provide a more comprehensive understanding of the genetic basis of color variation in potato and its plasticity across agroecological zones.
 
\end_layout

