\begin_layout Section
Conclusions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This genetic study of pigmentation traits in 
\shape italic
Andigenum
\shape default
 potato landraces from the CCC uncovered 21 significant SNP-trait associations spanning multiple plant organs,
 with 12 markers coinciding with previously characterized loci and nine representing novel associations that require experimental validation.
 The genetic architecture showed considerable overlap with genes from the 
\shape italic
Chilotanum
\shape default
 and 
\shape italic
Phureja
\shape default
 groups,
 confirming shared evolutionary pathways for the biosynthesis of pigments.
 However,
 43% of the significant markers were previously unreported,
 underscoring the unique genetic diversity of this underexplored germplasm.
 Color traits displayed moderate to high heritability (
\begin_inset Formula $h^{2}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $>$
\end_inset

 0.55 for primary colors),
 with genomic prediction achieving predictive abilities of up to 0.84.
 This demonstrates that marker-assisted selection is a viable strategy for incorporating color-associated nutritional traits into breeding programs.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Strong correlations between tuber pigmentation and antioxidant activity,
 total phenolic content,
 and ascorbic acid levels confirm the potential for developing nutritionally enhanced varieties through color trait selection.
 However,
 consumer acceptance remains a significant barrier despite their superior nutritional profiles,
 necessitating educational initiatives and market development strategies that recognize both cultural and nutritional values.
 These results emphasize the strategic importance of conserving and characterizing native potato genetic resources as sources of beneficial alleles to address global malnutrition challenges while preserving agricultural biodiversity and cultural heritage through 
\shape italic
ex situ
\shape default
 conservation and molecular breeding.
\end_layout

